Z||000000||HYPTNOTiSE||10-07-2000||02:36 AM||||I was driving a 97 Z-34 monte carlo today with the twin dual cam 3.4 and automatic. Anyway i was pulling away from a stop sign and i wanted to light the tires up, but instead of roasting em, it put the tranny in neutral(or something was slipping badly) and i sat there and bounced it off the rev limiter a couple times.  I looked down the the seleter was still in D3.  It did that twice on two seperate occasions. Seemed only to happen from  dead stops or when i was about to really roast the tires. is that a tranny saving device is or something messed up?  I thought i blew the tranny the first time it happened. No clunks or loud obvious noises, no  squeeling, no smell. Whats the deal?<P>------------------<BR>       Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ Nitrous<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/hypsig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||stsmithgt||10-07-2000||05:52 PM||||I had a ford C-4 tranny you could put in reverse going down the freeway and you would stay in drive. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I've dropped it in to first gear using the floor shifter, but it would only downshift to second.  Sometimes I wonder why I try these things out. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">  Curiosity killed the cat.........that got in front of me!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||10-07-2000||11:21 PM||||Likely something is going in the trans. The 4T60E had problems of dropping into neutral. That's certainly not an ECM "feature" kicking in. The shift solenoids can't select neutral.<P>Get it checked.||||reg||1
